# Direct production dependencies. **Edit this file, not requirements.txt.**
#
# `requirements.txt` beside it is generated from this one and pins the entire
# transitive closure, so rebuilding a given commit installs byte-identical
# versions on every platform. Regenerate after any change here:
#
#     uv pip compile --universal apps/api/requirements.in -o apps/api/requirements.txt
#
# `--universal` matters: the resolution carries environment markers, so one file
# serves both the Linux image production runs and the macOS the local gate runs on.

# Batch 61. Raised from 0.111.0, which pinned `starlette 0.37.2` and every remaining
# advisory with it. None of them is reachable here — the multipart DoS pair
# (CVE-2024-47874, CVE-2025-54121) needs a route that parses a form and there is none,
# CVE-2026-48818 is Windows-only, and CVE-2026-48710's Host-header path poisoning
# matters to apps doing path-based authorisation in middleware, which this one does not:
# auth is a per-route dependency. So this is hygiene, and it is pinned as a set because
# the resolution is forced — FastAPI 0.141 requires pydantic >= 2.9.
fastapi==0.141.1
uvicorn[standard]==0.30.1
sqlalchemy[asyncio]==2.0.31
asyncpg==0.29.0
alembic==1.13.2
bcrypt==4.1.3
pyjwt==2.13.0
# Pinned explicitly rather than left to fastapi's floor, so the advisory set this batch
# closes is visible in the file that records it.
starlette==1.6.0
python-dotenv==1.2.2
# Forced by fastapi 0.141 (>= 2.9). The upgrade is not free: it moves `HTTPBearer` to
# 401 for a credential-less caller (RFC 7235 — 403 is for an *authenticated* caller who
# is forbidden), and it changes the model introspection `tests/test_wire_datetimes.py`
# walks to prove Batch 43's UTC-offset guard still has a subject. Both are handled in
# this batch rather than absorbed.
pydantic==2.13.4
pydantic-settings==2.13.0
httpx==0.27.0
apscheduler==3.10.4
tzlocal==5.2
pywebpush==2.0.0
structlog==24.4.0
slowapi==0.1.9
# Batch 44. The API's only image decoder, and the reason profile pictures can be
# enabled at all: a magic-byte prefix proves a header, and re-encoding through a real
# imaging library is what neutralises the payload behind it. Kept on the newest patched
# line deliberately — this is the one dependency here whose whole job is parsing bytes
# a stranger chose.
pillow==12.3.0

# An unpinned transitive of pywebpush. Raised from `<=46.0.3` in Batch 59, on the
# instruction the old comment left behind: "if a CVE lands in the gap, raise this bound".
# Three did, and the premise the old bound rested on turned out to be wrong.
#
# **The premise was that cryptography never sees untrusted input here.** It does.
# `POST /push/subscribe` takes `keys` straight from the browser (`routers/notifications.py`),
# stores it, and `send_notification` hands it to `webpush()`, which parses the
# client-supplied `p256dh` as an EC public key. That is exactly the surface of
# GHSA-r6ph-v2qm-q3c2 — missing prime-order subgroup validation in `load_der_public_key`
# and `EllipticCurvePublicNumbers.public_key()`. Authenticated input, so bounded, but real.
#
# 48.0.1 is chosen, not the newest, and the reason is wheels rather than caution:
#
#   46.0.5  fixes the SECT-curve subgroup attack (the reachable one, above)
#   46.0.7  fixes a buffer overflow on non-contiguous buffers into Hash.update
#   48.0.1  ships an OpenSSL without the 2026-06-09 advisory statically linked
#   49.0.0  X.509 path-building blowup — this app validates no chains
#   49.0.0  wildcard-SAN escape in `cryptography.x509.verification` — same, no chain validation here
#   50.0.0  PKCS#7 EnvelopedData Bleichenbacher oracle — this app decrypts no PKCS#7
#
# Re-audited 2026-08-26 (docs/review/2026-08-26/04-operations.md, OPS-07): same three
# CVEs, same disposition. If the macOS-wheel gap ever closes (or a Rust toolchain
# becomes acceptable in CI), 49.0.0+ clears all three at once.
#
# So 48.0.1 clears everything reachable. It is also the last version publishing a macOS
# `universal2` wheel, which installs on Intel as well as Apple silicon; 49 and 50 publish
# no macOS wheel at all and would need a Rust toolchain to build on the local gate. The
# old comment said there was no Intel wheel above 46.0.3 — there is no *x86_64-tagged*
# one, but universal2 carries an x86_64 slice and installs fine.
cryptography==48.0.1
